# Keep the self-updated files byte-stable so Ed25519 signatures stay valid.
# git must NOT rewrite line endings for these, or agent.manifest.json.sig won't match
# the bytes raw.githubusercontent.com serves and the fleet will reject every update.
agent/agent.manifest.json -text
agent/agent.manifest.json.sig -text
# The beta channel's pair (roadmap #21). Pinned for exactly the same reason and BEFORE the
# first beta is published: a signature covers bytes, so a file that gets its line endings
# rewritten between signing and serving is indistinguishable from a tampered one, and the
# fleet would refuse the update with no obvious cause.
agent/agent.manifest.beta.json -text
agent/agent.manifest.beta.json.sig -text
# The client download catalogue (roadmap #11), signed with the same key. It ships under
# hub/ because the hub's self-updater mirrors that directory and nothing else.
hub/client.manifest.json -text
hub/client.manifest.json.sig -text
hub/client.manifest.beta.json -text
hub/client.manifest.beta.json.sig -text

# The Linux agent's shell script and systemd unit must reach a Linux box with LF endings.
# A .sh checked out with CRLF fails as `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`, which reads
# like a missing interpreter rather than a line-ending problem; a unit file with CRLF is
# accepted by systemd and then silently carries the ^M into every value it parses.
# Pinned rather than left to core.autocrlf, because the machine that checks this repo out
# to build a release is a Windows workstation.
agent-linux/**/*.sh text eol=lf
agent-linux/packaging/*.service text eol=lf

# Vendored browser dependencies are recorded by SHA-256 in hub/static/vendor/README.md, and a
# checksum is only the record of what is served if the bytes on disk are the bytes that were
# downloaded. core.autocrlf is true on the workstation that checks this repo out, and git
# classifies minified JS as text -- so without these pins every checksum in that table would
# drift on the next checkout, and `sha256sum ... # must match the table above` would fail for
# a file nobody had touched. Same reasoning as the signed manifests above, one layer down:
# there the bytes carry a signature, here they carry a published hash.
hub/static/vendor/*.js -text
hub/static/vendor/*.css -text
hub/static/vendor/fonts/** -text
